<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uttaramerur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uttaramerur is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Inscriptions on its Vaikuntha Perumal temple from the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parantaka-i">Parantaka I</a>, dated around 919–921, set out rules for choosing the members of the village assembly's committees by drawing lots, with qualifications of property, age and learning. They are a key source for local self-government in medieval south India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uttaramerur.jpg" alt="Uttaramerur" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sowri (Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uthiramerur_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Uthiramerur, as the town is now spelt, lies about 90 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>. It began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> settlement, which the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a> formally made a brahamdeya village around 750 CE, a gift, it is believed, to Vedic Brahmins. A tenth-century inscription calls it Uttaramerur Chaturvedi Mangalam. About 25 inscriptions span the reigns of some four Pallava kings, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, who took the region late in the 9th century, left records of gifts to the temples under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja Chola I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulothunga Chola I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest surviving records, of the Pallava king Dantivarman, show two assemblies: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabha-libya">Sabha</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> only, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, open to all classes. The Sabha met in the temple hall, was summoned by drums, sold land, kept a fund for dredging a tank and directed executive officers called variyars. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parantaka-i">Parantaka I</a> these officers gave way to committees, variyams, of 6 to 12 members, chosen each year from the village's 30 wards.</p><p>A nominee had to own tax-paying land of at least a quarter of a veli, or an eighth for one learned in a Veda and a Bhashya, live in a house on his own land, be aged 35 to 70 and know the mantras and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>. Recent committee members, those who had failed to submit accounts and those guilty of the great sins were excluded. Names written on palm leaves were put in a pot, the Kudavoloi, and a young boy drew them. Members served 360 days, and theft and forgery were punished by parading the offender on a donkey.</p><p>In the 13th century the village came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> authority, then under the Telugu Chola ruler Vijaya Gandagopala, who renamed it, and later the Vijayanagara emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> endowed its temples. From the 14th century political instability brought a steady decline in its agriculture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Uthiramerur is a panchayat town in Kancheepuram district, with a population of 25,194 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> are its main religions. Its temples include the Sundaravarada Perumal temple, about 1200 years old and built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a>, the Vaikunda Perumal temple and the Subramanya temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uthiramerur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Villages were run by assemblies, recorded in the <b>Uttaramerur</b> inscriptions, and land was carefully surveyed for revenue.</blockquote><a href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a></li></ul></section>
